\begin_inset Formula $\binom{n}{2}$
\end_inset

 specifies the range of hierarchy-model element pairs that must be considered.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Application of comparative judgement imposes an ordering that must subsequently be observed.
 Consider a hierarchy model 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}$
\end_inset

 comprised of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 elements,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}\supseteq\left\{ e_{1},\cdots,e_{n}\right\} $
\end_inset

.
 Comparative judgement 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{J}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 entails 
\begin_inset Formula $\binom{n}{2}$
\end_inset

 distinct mappings
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
(e_{i},e_{j}):\mathcal{J}_{\mathfrak{H}}\mapsto a_{i,j}\:\forall\,i\neq j\mbox{,}\label{eq:comparative-judgement-assignment}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i,j}$
\end_inset

 denotes a corresponding element of a reciprocal matrix.
 The mapping 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{J}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 imposes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{c}
e_{i}\succcurlyeq e_{j}\Rightarrow a_{i,j}\geq1\\
e_{i}\prec e_{j}\Rightarrow a_{i,j}<1
\end{array}\mbox{.}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
end{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset

Consequently,
 after its application,
 element ordering must be maintained.
 Finally,
 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i,j}$
\end_inset

 is postive-definite.
\end_layout

