) are completely general.
 That 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{\omega}_{O-C}$
\end_inset

 is a projection onto the unit simplex 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta^{3}$
\end_inset

 where
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\Delta^{n}=\left\{ \boldsymbol{x}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}\mid\sum_{i}x_{i}=1\;\wedge x_{i}\geq0\:\forall i\right\} 
\]

\end_inset

 is highly significant.
 All AHP calculation results are constrained to the interval 
\begin_inset Formula $\left[0,\,1\right]$
\end_inset

.
 This inlcudes both AHP-calculation results and standardization of inputs demonstrated in a subsequent subsection.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
On inconsistencies in comparative judgements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Glenn Shafer once opined,
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

...probability is not really about numbers;
 it is about the structure of reasoning...
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (quoted in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Pearl1988"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 p.
 15).
 The AHP methodology contains mechanisms to identify inconsistencies and contradictions in the structure of reasoning (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty2003,Pant2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 Here,
 we consider the types of inconsistencies in comparative judgements from which concerns might arise.
 We then review 
\emph on
consistency indices
\emph default
,
 AHP's mechanism to quantify the degree of inconsistency.
 Finally,
 we consider alternative graph-theoretic approaches to mitigate inconsistencies in reasoning.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Types of inconsistencies comparative judgements.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1986,Saaty2003"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 expressed concern about four types of inconsistencies that might adversely affect decision making aided by the AHP.
 Such opportunities particularly arise in comparative judgements.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty2003"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 seemingly suggested that accomodation of logical inconsistency in the AHP method might be more a feature than a bug:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

...a modicum of inconsistency may be considered as a good thing,
 and forced consistency without knowledge of the precise values as an undesirable compulsion.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The DQ framework strives for sound reasoning.
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Pant2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 articulates the goal of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

...a decision-making process with some constraints wherein the task is to obtain the maximum benefit from the available resources to get the best achievable results.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 The consequences to these goals arising from avoidable inconsistencies bear circumspectful consideration.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
Circular reasoning.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Expressing the first inconsistency scenario from 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty2003"
literal "false"

\end_inset

,
 equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:circular-reasoning-preference-ordering"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{c}
A\succ B\mbox{,}\\
B\succ C\mbox{, and}\\
C\succ A
\end{array}\label{eq:circular-reasoning-preference-ordering}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

exemplifies a preference ordering that contains circular reasoning.
 This obviously codifies a logical contradiction,
 violating the 
\emph on
completeness
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
transitivity
\emph default
 rational-preference constraints of the von-Neumann-Morgenstern axioms (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Kochenderfer2015"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 Furthermore,
 it also violates one of 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
refcite{deFinetti1974}{de Finetti's}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
refcite{deFinetti1974}{(1974)}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 principles of coherence:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

...no limits on the probabilities that an individual may assign,
 except 
\emph on
they must not be in contradiction among themselves
\emph default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (emphasis added).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
American mathematician William Thurston argued that 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Mathematics is not about numbers,
 equations,
 computations,
 or algorithms:
 
\emph on
It is about understanding
\emph default

\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (quoted in 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after "p. 76"
key "Cook2018"
literal "false"

\end_inset

;
 emphasis added).
 Applying computational machinery like AHP to a contradictory logical construct does not enhance understanding.
 It invites confusion.
 Alerting producers of contradictory comparative judgements and facilitating elimination of the circular-reasoning instances better serves the goals of sound reasoning (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Spetzler2016"
literal "false"

\end_inset

) and best-observable results (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Pant2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

