\begin_layout Paragraph*
Longitudinal comparative-judgement inconsistencies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally,
 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty2003"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 expresses concern that 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

...people would be required to be like robots unable to change their minds with new evidence...
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 Nothing elsewhere in the literature defining the AHP methodology proscribes such standard Bayesian updating (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1986,Saaty1987,Saaty1994"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 In fact,
 recent research explicitly adapts AHP to Bayesian patterns of reasoning (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Merrick2005,Altuzarra2007,LiH2025"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph*
Transitive inconsistencies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1986"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 defines a criterion for transitive consistency 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{c}
a_{i,j}\,a_{j,k}=a_{i,k}\\
\mbox{where}\:a_{i,j}=[\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}]_{i,j}
\end{array}\label{eq:transitive-judgement-consistency}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

for reciprocity matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 associated with hierarchy model 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathfrak{H}$
\end_inset

.
 This provides the basis for consistency indices.
 Now,
 (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:transitive-judgement-consistency"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

) imposes a stronger constraint than the von Neumann-Morgenstern transitivity axiom previously stated in (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:vonNeumann-Morgernstern-transitivity-1"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

),
 which only constrains transitivity of ordering preferences.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1986"
literal "false"

\end_inset

 contains an extensive set of definitions,
 axioms,
 and theorems culminating in the demonstration that,
 if the eigenvector/eigenvalue decomposition of a 
\emph on
consistent
\emph default
 reciprocal matrix 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
begin{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}\boldsymbol{X}_{\mathfrak{H}}=\boldsymbol{X}_{\mathfrak{H}}\boldsymbol{\Lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}}\mbox{,}\label{eq:general-reciprocal-eigendecomposition}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

then 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\lambda_{\mathfrak{H}}=n\label{eq:reciprocal-consistency-condition}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset ERT
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\backslash
end{subequations}
\end_layout

\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 denotes the order of 
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda_{\mathfrak{H}}=[\boldsymbol{\Lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}}]_{\text{argmax}(\boldsymbol{\Lambda}_{\mathfrak{H}})}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Transitive consistency bears important decision-theoretic consequences:
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

...when alternatives are scaled through paired comparisons,
 adding a new alternative can change the relative ranking of the old ones when the judgements are inconsistent or when several criteria are used
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Saaty1986"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
A consistency index for reciprocal matrices.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A consequence of (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:reciprocal-consistency-condition"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

),
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
CI=\frac{\lambda_{\mathfrak{H}}-n}{n-1}\label{eq:reciprocal-consistency-index}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

provides a commonly used 
\emph on
Consistency Index
\emph default
 for a reciprocal matrix
\begin_inset Formula $\boldsymbol{A}_{\mathfrak{H}}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "Pant2022"
literal "false"

\end_inset

).
 From (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:reciprocal-consistency-condition"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

),
 one obtains 
\begin_inset Formula $CI=$
\end_inset

0 for a perfectly consistent reciprocal matrix.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A 
\emph on
Consistency Ratio
\emph default

\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
CR=\frac{CI}{RI}\label{eq:reciprocal-consistency-ratio}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

accompanies the consistency index.
 In (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:reciprocal-consistency-ratio"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

